    "result_text": "Only the one test class exists (the other two files are support classes). The build passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`:\n\n- **New helper `CustomerCodes.java`** (`rest/controller/v1/`) \u2014 a small, cohesive class matching the existing `Namesakes`/`HouseholdDuplicates` pattern. Its `build(owner, existing)` method assembles the upper-cased 3-letter city prefix, the upper-cased 3-letter lastName prefix, and a per-city 4-digit sequence (count of existing owners in that same city, +1). Being a fresh single-purpose class, its surrounding complexity (`WMC_other`) is ~0.\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the three inline lines that built the old `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` code with a single `owner.setCustomerCode(CustomerCodes.build(owner, existingOwners))` call. This keeps the edit inside the large controller class down to one line and doesn't raise that method's cyclomatic complexity.\n\n- **`membershipNumber`** (in `OwnerMapper`) is derived by concatenating `owner.getCustomerCode()`, so it automatically rebuilds on the new format \u2014 `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>-M<YY>` \u2014 with no edit required, keeping the touched-file count at two.\n\nThe acceptance test (`customerCode` matches `[A-Z]{3}-[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}`) and the full suite pass: **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
